          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy the crimper that matches the terminals you actually use. For most toolboxes that is a ratcheting insulated-terminal crimper like the Klein 3005CR, with the Channellock 909 as the budget fallback. Add the 3006CR for bare terminals, the IWISS IWS-3220M for open-barrel pins, the Knipex 97 53 04 for ferrules, the Klein VDV226-110 for network cable and the TEMCo TH0006 for battery lugs only when the job calls for them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, the habits matter more than the brand: match the die to the terminal, let the ratchet finish every cycle, and pull-test the joint before it goes into service. A crimp that survives a hard tug on the bench will survive years of vibration in the field.
